// L4 `F` — are the two world types still TELLABLE APART, and does the compiler say so
// in words a reader can act on?
//
// ⚠ THE DIRECTION IS DELIBERATE: THEIR value into OUR function, not the reverse.
// Until `L6.2` this file went the other way — our world into their `cell_count` — and
// that reading is no longer available: `cell_count` is a METHOD on their `World`, and
// ours is `VoxelWorld` now, so it is not a candidate at all and the compiler answers
// `Unknown function cell_count`. True, and it says nothing about whether the two types
// merged. Handing THEIR world to OUR free function keeps both types in one diagnostic.
//
// Before `L6.2` the answer was the least useful sentence available:
//
//     error: expected World, got World
//
// — the `Surface` diagnostic of `L1`, verbatim, one rename later. Now it reads
//
//     error: expected VoxelWorld, got World
//
// which is the whole of what renaming the struct bought. The types never merged; what
// changed is that a reader can now see they did not.
use hex_voxel;
use hex_world;

fn main() {
  yours = world_empty();                                  // hex_world::World
  b = hex_voxel::world_has_chunk(yours, 0, 0);            // wants a hex_voxel::VoxelWorld
  println("PROBE our world_has_chunk accepted THEIR World: {b}");
}
